$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-8$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+2)(x-8)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054752 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b23c4da-7a8d-5076-a4e9-8bcde40bc088" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Table of Signs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d5689f5-3979-5ac6-845f-792062a15f7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292992} {:answer/id #uuid "357e14ed-911d-5817-8fdc-5373a446bf5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "357e14ed-911d-5817-8fdc-5373a446bf5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293160} :answer-field/id #uuid "20a56fc5-da89-503d-b3fc-83bd481f98dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294038} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4897f8a8-9042-5997-81d4-cc1c5451594f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293230} {:answer/id #uuid "9bbd6db8-2667-557c-b594-34531eb30104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bbd6db8-2667-557c-b594-34531eb30104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293547} :answer-field/id #uuid "90716124-afaa-5fad-b484-c4b3ae01e055" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294452} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ca36bf8-ba4f-5b39-a9a3-69579e0327a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293306} {:answer/id #uuid "f11e29e9-5a8c-5b31-8a05-aac895b8e8a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ca36bf8-ba4f-5b39-a9a3-69579e0327a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293306} :answer-field/id #uuid "97c9536e-46f9-5cd5-9342-46511da7345e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b62a3ee1-92b6-5cd1-8a67-1ee93c23fffd" :question/math-academy-id "q-278486" :question/problem "What are the missing entries in the sign table below?\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | {{field-1}} | $+$ |\n| $x-1$ | $-$ | {{field-2}} | $+$ |\n| $(x+5)(x-1)$ | $+$ | {{field-3}} | $+$ |" :question/worked-solution "On the interval $(-5,1),$ we can choose a test point ${x}_{1}=0.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =0+5=(+), \\\\ x-1 & =0-1=(-), \\\\ (x+5)(x-1) & =(+)\\times (-)=(-),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+5)(x-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083238 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70c149d8-3739-564c-97c5-6636860ad0c4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Polynomial Given Two Imaginary or Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fc3c4bb-149f-5ffc-97a4-e605c569a112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293002} {:answer/id #uuid "7c847aab-bc5d-5c8a-bb64-e369f3e92041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293431} {:answer/id #uuid "891b7b21-3dd3-5a5b-a070-71b374f714a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293465} {:answer/id #uuid "c769df52-89b1-54df-9eee-1096ad11021a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293704} {:answer/id #uuid "f3affec0-5e08-51d1-8608-2d04df1b9df5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c769df52-89b1-54df-9eee-1096ad11021a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293704} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e0f07fa-3f4d-54bc-a756-d828cde31d9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5595fbd6-585b-5625-b1c7-99ec2edfefea" :question/math-academy-id "q-28185" :question/problem "The quadratic polynomial $f(x)$ has real coefficients, has roots ${x}_{1}=6\\text{i}$ and ${x}_{2}=-6\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $1.$ Find $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our quadratic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}=6\\text{i}$ and ${x}_{2}=-6\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=1,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}) & =(1)(x-6\\text{i})(x-(-6\\text{i})) \\\\  & =(x-6\\text{i})(x+6\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-6\\text{i})(x+6\\text{i}) & ={x}^{2}-(6\\text{i})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-36{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)={x}^{2}+36.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115286 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0236acfd-59d1-5bc8-9e60-1c76ddc536f5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given a Complex Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b58c2a-9453-5ba0-8e30-d7ef6f50f331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292973} {:answer/id #uuid "1fe3b0ab-4035-5215-83f4-f675f25dae49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2x+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293065} {:answer/id #uuid "990cf70d-51d7-5d57-8f12-e3cb78178131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-x+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293536} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e7310d-cf13-5cce-ad72-793eca71cfde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293693} {:answer/id #uuid "cf236c96-ad3b-54fe-bf77-4c695ec85331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+x-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fe3b0ab-4035-5215-83f4-f675f25dae49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2x+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293065} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5b5aedb-f997-5a0b-816b-7a74825693b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b3e3d2a-46f3-5327-8b01-7846488f9aea" :question/math-academy-id "q-28215" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-1+\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $1,$ find the equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-1+\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-1-\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(-1+\\text{i}))(x-(-1-\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(-1+\\text{i})-x(-1-\\text{i})+(-1+\\text{i})(-1-\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-x\\text{i}+x+x\\text{i}+1-{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115358 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "51739685-2d53-5c9b-870b-b557ffdf2768" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of a Triangle by Solving for a Variable Given a Trigonometric Ratio of One Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1757e5f4-54b5-5159-9ab2-49bce96f3df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293023} {:answer/id #uuid "9cbbe909-d185-59d0-b912-c153b5d3e71b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293549} {:answer/id #uuid "a10e5fb3-4c1c-5b66-89cd-0598cd19bb77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293566} {:answer/id #uuid "a838bda5-a5e8-5f6a-a576-b0a05239ea5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293599} {:answer/id #uuid "ac38d79d-d749-555c-aea9-60b199533b57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1757e5f4-54b5-5159-9ab2-49bce96f3df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293023} :answer-field/id #uuid "331c0849-08b7-58b1-a991-ead4ea6bc11c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0a77aee-e012-5d50-a91c-7eccfeb5ef38" :question/math-academy-id "q-28266" :question/problem "![](images/48/48cb6a248ab3ce706f26dc2e84e2feb337d509041c8b9756ea32fb3817aa097f.png)\n\nGiven that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A)=5,$ calculate the area (in square units) of $△ABC$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A)=5.$ Notice that relative to $∠A,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AC}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{BC}{AC} \\\\ 5 & =\\frac{2x+4}{x-1} \\\\ 5(x-1) & =2x+4 \\\\ 5x-5 & =2x+4 \\\\ 3x & =9 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =2x+4=2(3)+4=10, \\\\ AC & =x-1=3-1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115360 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe83dedc-3034-5e3a-b546-dd57c0ccc6e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of a Triangle by Solving for a Variable Given an Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62fd2e1e-c894-56ff-bdad-a3d722d754fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293336} {:answer/id #uuid "a43f1763-d348-5fc3-86bb-5b3eb6a7220d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293576} {:answer/id #uuid "adfca619-4c50-55cf-bc35-84d9301f25e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293613} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b3bb07-e05e-5205-a1c1-5734a8c0a9eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293701} {:answer/id #uuid "f997b95f-a99a-5b77-b147-99948a12e498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a43f1763-d348-5fc3-86bb-5b3eb6a7220d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293576} :answer-field/id #uuid "4675c8e0-ebf4-57e8-b4fe-9fcb51121df9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e988752b-e095-56b3-bad9-106f43a3e05c" :question/math-academy-id "q-28271" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7dc806c646f1e9f5edbfef789fbdc9dfca2745c637906bd61eccb8072440e8c0.png)\n\nIn square units rounded to one decimal place, find the area of $△ABC$ shown above." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $m∠A={63}^{∘}.$ Notice that relative to $∠A,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AC}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{BC}{AC} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({63}^{∘}) & =\\frac{3x+2}{3x} \\\\ 3x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({63}^{∘}) & =3x+2 \\\\ 3x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({63}^{∘})-3x & =2 \\\\ 3x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({63}^{∘})-1) & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{3(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({63}^{∘})-1)} \\\\ x & =0.692\\,5…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =3x+2=3(0.692\\,5…)+2=4.077\\,6…, \\\\ AC & =3x=3(0.692\\,5…)=2.077\\,6….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}(2.077\\,6…)(4.077\\,6…) \\\\  & =4.236\\,0… \\\\  & ≈4.2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115286 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0236acfd-59d1-5bc8-9e60-1c76ddc536f5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given a Complex Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31d81fe9-d8bf-5033-a531-4c38c16343bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+16x-8=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293143} {:answer/id #uuid "51e57fe8-9a16-5417-bb16-0d24dc3d0496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-2x-16=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293263} {:answer/id #uuid "a7fc5ffa-e73f-568f-8fcc-6c7ef5303257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+8x+16=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293596} {:answer/id #uuid "cc23f64d-4c7f-5fcd-bb63-861fe1040ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-16x-8=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293721} {:answer/id #uuid "f7afe0ce-d392-5bc0-8fdd-9a850ab8beec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-2x+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7fc5ffa-e73f-568f-8fcc-6c7ef5303257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+8x+16=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293596} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ced79ac-f453-56c8-9912-7f811ed8d885" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "132a855d-cb67-58ff-b1b4-66591e373ee1" :question/math-academy-id "q-28273" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-1+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $4$, find the equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-1+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(-1+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}))(x-(-1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(-1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})-x(-1+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})+(-1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})(-1+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x+\\sqrt{3}x\\text{i}+x-\\sqrt{3}x\\text{i}+1-3{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=-1\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $4.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+2x+4) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+8x+16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110601 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f3d7d62-686b-59c4-9ea1-695c6be3180b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Unknown Parameters Given Two Perpendicular Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55f88508-da2b-5f50-bc9a-a9d26b49e411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293282} {:answer/id #uuid "a5105cfc-01bb-5d42-a770-d43d047ecbd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293581} {:answer/id #uuid "c8156b99-af7d-5f7b-b0a7-f383c4b1a5c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293707} {:answer/id #uuid "c91b613b-66f6-5d95-9a11-12d165d11100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293713} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ecb80b-49f9-59d8-9960-66dfad7c060e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8156b99-af7d-5f7b-b0a7-f383c4b1a5c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293707} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2a93840-02de-536d-9650-a5ee63bece85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe99c4a9-356e-5f86-a2a8-a7dd24fba9f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-28328" :question/problem "Given that the lines $y=5qx+9$ and $y=-3x-8$ are perpendicular, what is the value of $q?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nBoth lines are slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. \n\n- The slope of the line $y=5qx+9$ is $5q.$\n\n- The slope of the line $y=-3x-8$ is $-3.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n5q\\cdot (-3)=-1\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{1}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295269 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110600 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e732f270-1d6f-5ff8-8792-d9b329ea858d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Standard Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96da5639-b1ab-5764-ba59-7e12ce5f9525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{5}x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293524} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c3ebe1-76b4-534e-ac97-16940002c63a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}x-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293692} {:answer/id #uuid "d426e426-55ae-5a3a-aa02-c573670e3364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293751} {:answer/id #uuid "d741d61c-f686-5e74-9421-30cd24022c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293765} {:answer/id #uuid "fcbe027c-6c76-580a-9173-eaad9074aa07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d426e426-55ae-5a3a-aa02-c573670e3364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293751} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c87e56a-6a3b-50b2-a894-d68a942d6fc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7542d07b-ac7a-53fd-84e7-5ac011364c8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-28338" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $4x+10y=20?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $4x+10y=20,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+10y & =20 \\\\ 10y & =-4x+20 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{4}{10}x+\\frac{20}{10} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{5}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=\\frac{5}{2}x-4$ has a slope of $\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{2}{5})\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{5}{2}x-4$ is perpendicular to the given line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66534e01-fdc6-522f-b90a-59a8118bc67b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Complex Root of a Quadratic Equation Given the First Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074e1768-bc8a-50d9-b53c-43bd12be6b05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-2-7\\text{i}}{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295303} {:answer/id #uuid "26f0903e-3e78-51bc-b188-e2eb5a092c40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-7\\text{i}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295479} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e89224-8057-5f39-9fb8-31f4cb3fe5a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-7-2\\text{i}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296398} {:answer/id #uuid "da9cbed8-b43d-563e-851e-a996cab9c5de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-2-7\\text{i}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296420} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b95dbe-7364-5af0-8a0d-dc48365dde9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-7+2\\text{i}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da9cbed8-b43d-563e-851e-a996cab9c5de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-2-7\\text{i}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296420} :answer-field/id #uuid "49a20f34-443b-55d2-af98-8273be8b8ace" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "179b51f5-6286-53a0-bd97-91549ab321d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-28519" :question/problem "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a root $x=\\frac{-2+7\\text{i}}{2}.$ What is the other root?" :question/worked-solution "We're given that the quadratic equation has real coefficients and that $x=\\frac{-2+7\\text{i}}{2}=-1+\\frac{7}{2}\\text{i}$ is a root. Therefore, its complex conjugate $x=-1-\\frac{7}{2}\\text{i}=\\frac{-2-7\\text{i}}{2}$ is also a root."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0db14c2a-b6c3-5550-8ff6-c1a9e2b4ea5d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given an Imaginary Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bad7587-7ec6-57a4-a5f4-b297e751b2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295331} {:answer/id #uuid "651357c3-155d-5e2b-8229-a2f0c8d73b99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295843} {:answer/id #uuid "afeb9aa0-d6c7-567a-841d-74f1e5863e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}-12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296194} {:answer/id #uuid "bdeabb6f-d608-5568-a961-832dfff28339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296268} {:answer/id #uuid "f775651a-d43f-5122-bc88-ae7cb6a50b17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "651357c3-155d-5e2b-8229-a2f0c8d73b99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295843} :answer-field/id #uuid "5098eb42-9a92-5a11-971d-950db7f70c28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6f01fd2-19c8-5c6d-b91d-8da801444f5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-28523" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=2\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $3$, find the equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=2\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=-2\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-2\\text{i})(x+2\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm 2\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $3.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}^{2}+4) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+12 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f65f58-bd3d-5c3f-b9e0-462d74d75b59" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b268833-a892-5a14-ab75-38558f16bc57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295511} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7be3a8-94b4-5a24-adb6-b753140ab093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296278} {:answer/id #uuid "d19d31e7-9654-53a0-8391-14a1f9103d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296372} {:answer/id #uuid "d427a2bc-12c0-5bdc-b3d1-7a4dbeb358d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296383} {:answer/id #uuid "edf73d42-1443-5359-8707-64a0a6a850c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d427a2bc-12c0-5bdc-b3d1-7a4dbeb358d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296383} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0375b64-7629-58c5-bdc5-3b40af6de210" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "88cefa50-35a4-5398-bbcf-d41d22641c1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-28556" :question/problem "Find ${g}^{″}(4)$ if $g(x)=4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{x}-2x+7.$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={(4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{x}-2x+7)}^{′} \\\\  & ={(4{x}^{5/2}-2x+7)}^{′} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{5}{2}){x}^{3/2}-2+0 \\\\  & =10{x}^{3/2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(x) & ={(10{x}^{3/2}-2)}^{′} \\\\  & =10(\\frac{3}{2}){x}^{1/2}-0 \\\\  & =15{x}^{1/2} \\\\  & =15\\sqrt{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the second derivative at $x=4$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(4) & =15\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =15(2) \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6cd2f04-e0d5-5df0-8e74-ea737501cdf8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ca1cf7-eae8-588f-b62e-92e2eed7ae06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295403} {:answer/id #uuid "5f7c70a7-d1b8-56a0-9961-6101a0fea82a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295813} {:answer/id #uuid "89099718-058c-53aa-ac2e-3dc7b59c4b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296004} {:answer/id #uuid "a1cb3a02-6977-565b-8c6f-64a04e91a8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296129} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf45a98-a317-5ebe-a0ee-d58863e54e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16ca1cf7-eae8-588f-b62e-92e2eed7ae06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295403} :answer-field/id #uuid "78f9540e-726b-5906-87cd-68bbcee4ccc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "573bc8f1-012d-5d7e-834a-c6ec17bb0b63" :question/math-academy-id "q-287970" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2px=3x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=2p,\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n2p=3.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204090 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0e7426e-553d-5f20-811f-15f3a5ea0056" :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a9cd574-87f6-5684-a4a2-52f23dcc4f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295323} {:answer/id #uuid "4512c30d-0418-5d1a-a9a4-5767ffaddbf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295659} {:answer/id #uuid "4e445b8e-50d5-51d4-9056-86f12aba889d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295718} {:answer/id #uuid "967246bb-eb10-50c8-9141-55173f83e32f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296068} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ae615f-ae70-595f-a21b-63e9d4cc1379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2ae615f-ae70-595f-a21b-63e9d4cc1379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296210} :answer-field/id #uuid "078b8df7-1fda-503a-8213-ba030a04d1b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9d84e977-5f76-5e6a-86a0-a01877993e64" :question/math-academy-id "q-288143" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n-3(q+4)x=6\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=b.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=-3(q+4),\\,b=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n-3(q+4)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3(q+4) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-3(q+4)}{-3} & =\\frac{0}{-3} \\\\ q+4 & =0 \\\\ q & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297180 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213003 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5cf64df7-0abb-5b42-bed6-b3664c2de89a" :knowledge-point/title "Matching a Single Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "381c6cc6-b38f-5f53-b305-453281ca3851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295581} {:answer/id #uuid "60e8b5fe-246c-568f-92d9-946f0504fb9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295824} {:answer/id #uuid "77d2ee40-a49f-5b7b-8c75-007236a13614" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295922} {:answer/id #uuid "93a6f32e-a6a3-5689-96e3-737573f92b3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296057} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c8bcbd-fd03-58ad-896a-0505d8219e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4c8bcbd-fd03-58ad-896a-0505d8219e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296304} :answer-field/id #uuid "a549f3f8-6e62-5582-a6e8-36a320f44157" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d2e83b2-fdb4-5969-995c-ff8567f8cf5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-288406" :question/problem "The following equation is true for all values of $x.$\n\n$$\n-5{x}^{2}+mx-6=-5{x}^{2}-3x-6\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of the constant $m?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nWe compare the linear terms on both sides. On the left-hand side, the coefficient of $x$ is $m.$ On the right-hand side, the coefficient of $x$ is $-3.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nm=-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6cd2f04-e0d5-5df0-8e74-ea737501cdf8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45491d55-84a5-5641-85ee-02ca1a1e90b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295660} {:answer/id #uuid "7b982f8e-1429-5175-9e47-06c33c917451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295944} {:answer/id #uuid "9130ffb1-ff3d-56f6-b44a-a46263f888e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296047} {:answer/id #uuid "9540e0f1-3b23-5210-976d-85a05ecc13df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296063} {:answer/id #uuid "f61f19b9-1bc9-5327-81ed-c1e60a23106f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9540e0f1-3b23-5210-976d-85a05ecc13df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296063} :answer-field/id #uuid "057a3936-10b0-5e7b-83aa-558ff78363fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "556d6e5b-c151-55e9-9606-63f27cad6bd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-288436" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n5px=10x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=5p,\\,b=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5p=10.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213004 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2caeb2c-39cf-595a-8105-67d11bf0e45e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Two Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a121ddd-d30b-57ab-802c-d2a28aedc332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295598} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2d0db4-00f9-5688-a436-3938b8bbd7af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295617} {:answer/id #uuid "902f311b-9a33-5eca-947d-790debf8ade1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296040} {:answer/id #uuid "93583915-e4a1-5ab4-acc2-719563e5af95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296056} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2391b3-6270-5d08-8a37-4f5e7d3097e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a121ddd-d30b-57ab-802c-d2a28aedc332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295598} :answer-field/id #uuid "8913da08-c2a3-53da-a58d-4cb84b000f0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12cdfe66-5367-5a52-9239-ea7358c10305" :question/math-academy-id "q-288452" :question/problem "The equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+nx+d={x}^{2}-12x-8\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $n$ and $d$ are constants. What is the value of $n+d?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's compare the coefficients:\n\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $n,$ and on the right-hand side it is $-12.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nn=-12.\n$$\n\n- The constant term on the left-hand side is $d,$ and on the right-hand side it is $-8.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nd=-8.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n+d & =-12+(-8) \\\\  & =-12-8 \\\\  & =-20\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213005 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46822f28-8863-5150-a420-09c61a5c28b1" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Factored Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02184017-3e72-54aa-bfde-e944921a8b7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295273} {:answer/id #uuid "16eb5901-201a-51bf-b087-292957bc4fd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295404} {:answer/id #uuid "6c4db019-1fa9-5008-a1ff-18e087f00500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295869} {:answer/id #uuid "afa15348-38f9-5b8f-840e-cdf2428bbe13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296192} {:answer/id #uuid "e425cd77-1bed-5dc0-8e91-a77932df18a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afa15348-38f9-5b8f-840e-cdf2428bbe13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296192} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f05b554-7726-5f32-a015-9268ca6b0c9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58c900de-ded0-51c6-94b4-0ca8530168c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-288468" :question/problem "The equation\n\n$$\nt(2{x}^{2}-3x+1)=-6{x}^{2}+9x-3\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $t$ is a constant. What is the value of $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means that after distributing $t$ on the left-hand side, the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's expand the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nt(2{x}^{2}-3x+1)=2t{x}^{2}-3tx+t\n$$\n\nThis must equal the right-hand side. So, we have\n\n$$\n2t{x}^{2}-3tx+t=-6{x}^{2}+9x-3.\n$$\n\nNow, we compare the coefficients of corresponding terms. \n\nComparing the ${x}^{2}$ terms, we have\n\n$$\n2t=-6\\,⇒\\,t=-3.\n$$\n\nLet's verify that value $t=-3$ produces the correct linear coefficient and constant term:\n\n- $-3t=(-3)(-3)=9\\,✓$\n\n- $t=-3\\,✓$\n\nSo, our solution is valid."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204090 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0e7426e-553d-5f20-811f-15f3a5ea0056" :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "298bad1f-9367-5a05-95d2-933a09daee2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295501} {:answer/id #uuid "3dad73b7-975d-5246-83b7-9657d612712e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295623} {:answer/id #uuid "a52921f6-35e3-5fd6-831b-5c76a68fe597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296138} {:answer/id #uuid "c10c0188-b833-5221-be57-af73a4dae17b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296286} {:answer/id #uuid "f34978c9-f088-5aab-b438-960ba47ee1ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a52921f6-35e3-5fd6-831b-5c76a68fe597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296138} :answer-field/id #uuid "aab0add4-30b0-59ee-8400-8a7cc1beaf29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1328ee8c-fdcc-59c5-a877-6291af207d84" :question/math-academy-id "q-288596" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $y,$ given by\n\n$$\n(q+9)y=-6\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ay=b.$ In this case,\n\n$$\na=q+9,\\,b=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nq+9=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q+9 & =0 \\\\ q & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b1fd794-f42b-5316-80e9-0698cb1f8b87" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a General Equation With Infinitely Many Solutions For Unknown Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26dea449-5daa-571d-bc24-21b16885571b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295478} {:answer/id #uuid "46ff9640-80f1-5703-b7a0-6219bb604868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295672} {:answer/id #uuid "7f368c30-e2fd-5534-8546-adacd3bdc62c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295955} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0e5e07-5208-59c1-8031-79b63eb57a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296248} {:answer/id #uuid "e8efa7fd-e32d-5724-96bb-c481dd8ea2c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46ff9640-80f1-5703-b7a0-6219bb604868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295672} :answer-field/id #uuid "049c5b1b-3107-5777-ad7f-5a1a5ac925c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "afc322c5-285c-5560-a114-d1da26bf18af" :question/math-academy-id "q-288622" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(m+2)x+5n=7x+15\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ and $n$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $m+n?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $m$ and $n$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(m+2)x+5n=7x+15\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+2=7.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n5n=15.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $m=5$ and $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+n=5+3=8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b1fd794-f42b-5316-80e9-0698cb1f8b87" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a General Equation With Infinitely Many Solutions For Unknown Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28da790f-20c9-5875-b417-1be764fa2aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295496} {:answer/id #uuid "8db21277-c307-578a-8ebf-d5c8733da1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296020} {:answer/id #uuid "c24c121c-9aae-5ae4-989f-173c011f7cdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296294} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c8cfa6-04bf-59ad-b50b-09698209ddbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296316} {:answer/id #uuid "e7994d65-33f9-5bec-9e19-0969a17a2b2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8db21277-c307-578a-8ebf-d5c8733da1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296020} :answer-field/id #uuid "a71ebbc6-abec-50c8-b8f1-d671ed8c0cf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f55693d-3dd0-5310-a538-5dcb7f69af97" :question/math-academy-id "q-288627" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{2}x+q=4x+20\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ and $q$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p+q?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $p$ and $q$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{2}x+q=4x+20\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\nq=20.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $p=8$ and $q=20,$ we have\n\n$$\np+q=8+20=28.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083388 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c16a30bc-0c1a-54f2-95d6-47edb0c85736" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Product of the Roots of a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "119dc29c-b137-50a6-9c2e-9ac8d5e9af59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295367} {:answer/id #uuid "3082a4a4-01f9-5e69-8589-4ae793f7d894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295545} {:answer/id #uuid "60e31fc5-d679-52f0-8403-e834f9c90e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295823} {:answer/id #uuid "e3fabf97-650c-5f20-8a3e-869f773274b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296483} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5f880e-5916-5bea-a034-1eba1b1f15a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60e31fc5-d679-52f0-8403-e834f9c90e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295823} :answer-field/id #uuid "888fe82e-2f83-5273-a943-a23e7c4d783d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ed9536d-f38c-5295-8805-abb310c76773" :question/math-academy-id "q-288814" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n7{x}^{2}-5x-14=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=7,\\,b=-5,\\,c=-14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{(-14)}{7}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083388 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c16a30bc-0c1a-54f2-95d6-47edb0c85736" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Product of the Roots of a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00d74572-6c12-570d-b5b4-46d60457646d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295271} {:answer/id #uuid "19213a87-0bbb-5148-95b8-feaba74b4642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295415} {:answer/id #uuid "411e71ab-c2ca-55a2-b282-fec52773fc0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295640} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ca4c2e-cc61-5e4f-9b7c-53692845d61c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296390} {:answer/id #uuid "daf7dbe6-df28-5cec-bd96-e118b9a97488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5ca4c2e-cc61-5e4f-9b7c-53692845d61c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296390} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b1e993e-30b6-5f0e-8d28-4a1411fe2c2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2145d449-33d6-569e-b25a-b8ddea914c99" :question/math-academy-id "q-288815" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+7x-27=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=9,\\,b=7,\\,c=-27.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{(-27)}{9}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083388 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c16a30bc-0c1a-54f2-95d6-47edb0c85736" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Product of the Roots of a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79894418-732c-58c9-9ddb-c28b8aa917f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295931} {:answer/id #uuid "c7374353-ec78-5263-b05c-f6719c421c59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296313} {:answer/id #uuid "dcff367f-1190-560a-bb1e-d83c05126b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296439} {:answer/id #uuid "fb46df49-667f-5745-8cbf-7537238ae05d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296604} {:answer/id #uuid "fef8aec2-f84b-51b7-bcb0-0ca5c486d888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fef8aec2-f84b-51b7-bcb0-0ca5c486d888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296625} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1dc5883-eb26-5861-aa29-b6e4365854e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f379d5ac-6fe2-5ce8-b120-ae7f8d1580fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-288816" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-17x-30=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=6,\\,b=-17,\\,c=-30.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{(-30)}{6}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083388 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c16a30bc-0c1a-54f2-95d6-47edb0c85736" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Product of the Roots of a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a1d95b8-6f28-5ebe-ae92-d043359fa511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,395" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390574} {:answer/id #uuid "eed1e1d6-f081-50ba-9a0f-960b330272be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2)\\cdot 8\\,395