b/ref 17592186297216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083388 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c16a30bc-0c1a-54f2-95d6-47edb0c85736" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Product of the Roots of a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2567084b-b026-5eb4-b95e-0227a0635f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "391" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295471} {:answer/id #uuid "32c4576b-1351-5ac7-8694-de0426c7c366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-391" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295554} {:answer/id #uuid "71a3851f-e88d-5f38-b01f-fcaaad95d68b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-391}{41}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295893} {:answer/id #uuid "922397a2-e0b6-58f1-bde5-54e22966b2c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2)\\cdot 391" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296054} {:answer/id #uuid "abf37a1f-02b7-5b10-aaaf-f70acb95ee5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32c4576b-1351-5ac7-8694-de0426c7c366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-391" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295554} :answer-field/id #uuid "29b0ff42-3a4a-57e5-b225-3f319bf73f19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "edeac1f1-6a23-50a2-b07f-f8b62b91f0ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-288825" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+41x-391=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=1,\\,b=41,\\,c=-391.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{(-391)}{1}=-391.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115179 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec3e2ddd-4937-5837-a03a-b7568bff9b87" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7eb7a0-9e0b-5f24-9b6e-32d423c91838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295328} {:answer/id #uuid "23b932bc-94d0-5090-8141-409a5663c61e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295461} {:answer/id #uuid "4b71a211-d079-5dee-8bfc-012004c98779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295696} {:answer/id #uuid "a88803b8-c592-548a-a2d0-3411a63af750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296152} {:answer/id #uuid "fb317109-fe2c-5752-aced-8c8fe96089ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b71a211-d079-5dee-8bfc-012004c98779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295696} :answer-field/id #uuid "36407ae9-d72f-5658-821c-790738a7cf02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b48b10b3-0d9d-5485-bda2-3198c7563d19" :question/math-academy-id "q-28902" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1=3$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1 & =3 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](images/31/318eba038feb091d6e3adb80ddf325de6039bb331a7713cf1e2c02171a6388d4.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110667 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7ef1dd-58cc-57f5-9ff9-fc997f4280ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d716c0-c74a-57f6-833b-a5816bf99cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295386} {:answer/id #uuid "67139bf8-96d1-5cee-b658-3ffcabbeb7f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295848} {:answer/id #uuid "68c9ab76-11b5-597a-b277-a022ff7d7ce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295856} {:answer/id #uuid "8406301a-c3a9-5c90-b099-4f4b2c78ba3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295973} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a38ae3-a755-5134-b132-4a013f680737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14d716c0-c74a-57f6-833b-a5816bf99cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295386} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cc8c0d6-c061-513b-98bf-4315a1ac025c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4102b2c-f2ad-56ac-9dd6-7dd59719b972" :question/math-academy-id "q-28973" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}?$\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n- Its range is $y\\in (0,\\infty )$\n- It is a decreasing function" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of ${(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/c1/c1bbffed32708d30355ed3592deae845d1a84cd1f1772ef1d0fae7ac39518295.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f(x)={(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}$ is defined for all input values $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)={(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers.\n\n- Statement III is true. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I, II, and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110667 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7ef1dd-58cc-57f5-9ff9-fc997f4280ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d7bb9f1-b157-599d-93ed-6607f6670caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295620} {:answer/id #uuid "57f33323-a574-5d3a-9709-4eb09849a56b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295774} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d54f7c-6d02-5810-afd3-16ba3cfaf37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296154} {:answer/id #uuid "bba10e7d-2cc2-5341-a2f3-b9fb123d6f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296250} {:answer/id #uuid "e870b774-8e7e-5746-ab82-10ba9e3d3195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d7bb9f1-b157-599d-93ed-6607f6670caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295620} :answer-field/id #uuid "543e005e-a445-5e44-847a-061f1c3bcb23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c7808ae-1f8f-5217-b026-8ee08973156b" :question/math-academy-id "q-28981" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)=(0.08)^{x}?$\n\n- Its range is $y>0$\n- It is an increasing function\n- $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of ${(0.08)}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/b4/b4e3b5119c92d02bbceb786fefd238182c7c84ca0f4abc85a19f2df86a6ba267.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)={(0.08)}^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers.\n\n- Statement II is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115182 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "145bdcf2-b1d5-54a8-bd95-4b054d7a33f0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1368cfff-7db3-5254-96f3-07c2d868775c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295373} {:answer/id #uuid "5d31a2e7-f845-5d6f-84d8-734c7f9fdf5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295805} {:answer/id #uuid "6430dd94-cdd6-5b7c-a6ed-698e9ed44979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295838} {:answer/id #uuid "b6bfc3d8-2c65-5bf3-b9cc-a536405604d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296226} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4268bd-fe81-503e-a2c7-155bbc6c537d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d31a2e7-f845-5d6f-84d8-734c7f9fdf5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295805} :answer-field/id #uuid "52eff59e-5bce-5d06-bed1-69123a95aebf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "710ba61e-2523-50bc-83d0-e27883d20d99" :question/math-academy-id "q-29004" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\text{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the  first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](images/4c/4cbf970747a9eddbcffe6fd3ba46d13c43e5f17ca56e52747c797229dbc74d48.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203961 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58c7cd44-30a9-5f61-822c-13640ad41c93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Zeros of a Transformed Sine or Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b1117d8-3922-58c9-9530-2863e9f66fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295326} {:answer/id #uuid "1bf04524-d8f1-5e7a-91a7-879af77ff5ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295431} {:answer/id #uuid "3c4a9f04-2536-5359-9635-1fd6075b188e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295612} {:answer/id #uuid "94fd6bb9-96bf-57e3-9aa7-5130e61029b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296062} {:answer/id #uuid "d478ba2a-8dd5-55b8-a559-1c7c60934547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d478ba2a-8dd5-55b8-a559-1c7c60934547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296386} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9270dcb-2a83-5da1-b03e-a2f7992cae9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cc77154-ecae-5ece-8bcc-9eb3f418b440" :question/math-academy-id "q-29132" :question/problem "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{45}^{∘})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=x+{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of \n\n$$\ny=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x+{45}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x+{45}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘},$ where $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115705 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115181 :knowledge-point/id #