math} :answer/value "18\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390583} :answer-field/id #uuid "974b5aa7-9854-5922-a33e-647715b7b09b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20eb540b-84ee-5a09-81e9-e0f110dad1d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-29179" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2$ for $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNote that $x={60}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={60}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/9c/9c8adbb0a1594ae5bacfab7f1f92993c67659839599e10ab46d5cf98896e3ec7.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $X={300}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & ={300}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{60}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-{60}^{∘},-{300}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-60\\cdot (-300)=18\\,000\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115180 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28062dbf-815b-5221-ae86-1050e106c5ea" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b21c9bca-5889-5378-95f5-dc19174253a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390587} {:answer/id #uuid "5637088d-2110-5861-a9e4-5418d97814d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,175" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390588} {:answer/id #uuid "98625043-9024-52ca-9b27-4d102460e169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390589} {:answer/id #uuid "a3127733-14dd-544e-9920-fd0e17095202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390590} {:answer/id #uuid "510c63c8-8440-585e-ac63-db0b2b6359b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98625043-9024-52ca-9b27-4d102460e169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390589} :answer-field/id #uuid "c557a89c-b4c6-5e67-988d-6c588aefa937" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23dbd8da-da31-5df7-bb91-fad8d1a7bceb" :question/math-academy-id "q-29182" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})={30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNote that $x={30}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={30}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/8c/8c6e3ae6bdbfb1c66aee719f77225453992fffe6f3c6e3dfdc3f1ff92c59863a.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $X={330}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required dommain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={330}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-{30}^{∘},-{330}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-30\\cdot (-330)=9\\,900\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115184 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba963ded-e9ff-5324-9d22-acb6b7a85e10" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "285b16e3-c1a0-528c-aa3c-6503ddc7d9c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295491} {:answer/id #uuid "87b96519-0b71-53f7-a2a8-38e23e3ed540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295998} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc605ec-311a-5e45-aa11-fa47080ee6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296021} {:answer/id #uuid "a73b3e93-a76c-5d8c-952d-0ca55c712a69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296147} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc07055-c07b-591e-bb1f-6c27db9c5cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8dc605ec-311a-5e45-aa11-fa47080ee6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296021} :answer-field/id #uuid "38ba5974-6a88-54e6-877e-8ae9daa987cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e82d568-df50-578f-b07c-119397489f57" :question/math-academy-id "q-29207" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=1$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\text{arctan}(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{4}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129101 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8854eaba-96c7-5ea8-a071-7ff7c954f943" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Expression for the Asymptotes of a Transformed Tangent or Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8266d9da-29ef-5142-889c-67032e696941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295969} {:answer/id #uuid "8f36dd3b-18aa-5423-9a63-6be24cb45a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296030} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a3b5aa-10fe-59e1-8b60-91383fc7c7a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296242} {:answer/id #uuid "ce3fe793-587a-5cb1-82ef-b60494e08387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296347} {:answer/id #uuid "f5728d81-5718-5a99-b836-93226b4b6cc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9a3b5aa-10fe-59e1-8b60-91383fc7c7a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296242} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d308903-8122-5b47-a276-3957b8fde22e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "493ac61e-d5aa-548d-a691-2dfa99e52431" :question/math-academy-id "q-29349" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)-2,$ where $x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)-2,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(2x).$ This gives:\n\n$$\n2x={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSolving this for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401948 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115222 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80449399-3ae4-593f-8c3b-00bcdcc99727" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Outside of the Range of Cosecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b53db17c-c360-5669-8f88-e93f4bdbd8fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401950} {:answer/id #uuid "25dcd0ff-82e2-5596-97c0-808147eeda5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401951} {:answer/id #uuid "c06a013e-9247-5fe8-9462-4797376bce04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401952} {:answer/id #uuid "5d5c203d-08c8-5beb-ba1e-76f154f20aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401953} {:answer/id #uuid "12121f61-eccf-5393-b245-c6404140579b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25dcd0ff-82e2-5596-97c0-808147eeda5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401951} :answer-field/id #uuid "8895f00a-eea4-5d81-b69d-a26ada8cb409" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c11092b-9e53-51e1-b299-c5287bf677e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-29477" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\nI. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{3}$\nII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=3$\nIII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation I has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129100 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fabd8865-cc14-508a-aa81-4ba147f7f8d4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Expression for the Zeros of a Transformed Tangent or Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53c921c9-46e0-5bef-95de-0f5b588ebca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295749} {:answer/id #uuid "85d07a4f-56af-578a-a679-6e632e293884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295983} {:answer/id #uuid "8c7e58fb-f78c-5c59-97d6-77d4f59a186a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296016} {:answer/id #uuid "ab14ced1-375e-5455-909c-7a3ced2bd998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296164} {:answer/id #uuid "ee73340c-4d12-5b82-9ec4-7452a202940f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab14ced1-375e-5455-909c-7a3ced2bd998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296164} :answer-field/id #uuid "79362fdd-d341-5e89-bee7-6cb695806517" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "670b4725-4f5f-5e5b-81ee-7bebf6dbdf85" :question/math-academy-id "q-29556" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the zeros of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The zeros of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by\n\n$$\nx=n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of $f(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =n\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176049 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241c7bd-4a2b-556f-be9f-8a884addebb9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064a647e-d06a-50bb-9cbc-0f17389eca42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295298} {:answer/id #uuid "1066d92d-62c9-5dad-8b4c-dbbe71386d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295362} {:answer/id #uuid "3f3a0291-6ad9-5566-9fd5-0de08933b4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295630} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8ba8e5-5fce-5a75-b9a3-f9b2aab87da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296026} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2ff4e9-ee34-51e6-b267-06c09d19013f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e8ba8e5-5fce-5a75-b9a3-f9b2aab87da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296026} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1618392-44be-533c-81a8-efd84eeee3ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c5a3a079-0ec8-5b33-84d2-568517288149" :question/math-academy-id "q-29608" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=tan⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ represents any integer.\n\n- The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi .$\n- $tan⁡(x)=tan⁡(x+2n\\pi ).$\n- The range of the function is $[-1,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has period $\\pi .$ So, $tan⁡(x+2n\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi +n\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi )=tan⁡(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The range of $y=tan⁡x$ is $(-\\infty ,+\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115185 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c9c0f6-034c-5d9c-935a-ca27dec1a648" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35051668-a56c-5024-9510-5bb50d0360fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\,741" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395806} {:answer/id #uuid "9c07c6a6-5b40-55a4-9250-674b5ab51617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\,731" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395807} {:answer/id #uuid "0d4cbb58-b903-5380-9c08-172acb79c3a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\,631" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395808} {:answer/id #uuid "6fbfb4e9-a306-5d6f-a040-1f5b9ed69042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\,851" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395809} {:answer/id #uuid "8c3be72d-8de9-5e24-a778-ee5537402bbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\,561" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35051668-a56c-5024-9510-5bb50d0360fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\,741" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395806} :answer-field/id #uuid "4440a234-4417-5c7c-aab8-361ac22515f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0fa4866c-44a3-53be-9ea4-1b3c073da31b" :question/math-academy-id "q-29664" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x<{180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{4}{3})=(-53.130\\,1…)^{∘}≈-{53.130}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-{53.130}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-{53.130}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add and subtract integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+x={180}^{∘}-{53.130}^{∘}={126.870}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-{53.130}^{∘},{126.870}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n-53.130\\cdot 126.870≈-6\\,741\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1113b2b6-ac10-5599-9e94-5bbed95e32a5" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Definite Integral of a Radical Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "144a6036-1a43-5c55-bd4d-8c3b921ab81d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4-\\sqrt{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295382} {:answer/id #uuid "1a4bc189-56e8-506b-afae-8c32583fd169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4+\\sqrt{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295423} {:answer/id #uuid "4809f8aa-d435-582b-a813-64757ceea05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4-\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295678} {:answer/id #uuid "598e8e48-0407-5a12-b4c7-8a90c76b22d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295784} {:answer/id #uuid "addb0989-293c-52f5-817b-1eac8428806e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4+\\sqrt{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "144a6036-1a43-5c55-bd4d-8c3b921ab81d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4-\\sqrt{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295382} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0a2dd69-37ef-5e8e-92ea-d1b66a49d028" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "380886ef-0fbf-54bc-9579-e4e21dd4f7ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-29673" :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5/2+1}}{-\\frac{5}{2}+1}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-3/2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{3}}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{1}{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}-\\frac{1}{(\\sqrt{1})^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4-\\sqrt{2}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064196 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67bbd172-1940-5a1e-82f8-ffc73058f009" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Definite Integral of a Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a6f3f8e-0c5f-5742-981c-efe44d651035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "189" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295321} {:answer/id #uuid "7306755f-204c-5784-9675-85e4c40d4fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295896} {:answer/id #uuid "97ff1ca8-cab0-5552-a6be-a28c36723d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296080} {:answer/id #uuid "9cbdd0be-cf59-5728-817c-107e52a30f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{189}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296104} {:answer/id #uuid "ce989bf2-91f9-5cfb-894e-5fff41cce741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7306755f-204c-5784-9675-85e4c40d4fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295896} :answer-field/id #uuid "29935fac-8196-589a-b966-f9c10dd6483e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1ea9ad4a-c4e1-5eef-bc95-8f5cd98ebf22" :question/math-academy-id "q-29744" :question/problem "Evaluate  ${\\int }_{-5}^{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{2+1}}{2+1}{|}_{-5}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-5}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-5)^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}+\\frac{125}{3} \\\\  & =63.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105715 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf98ee24-7774-5674-8a1e-63f4f6d225c0" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "155da407-c8ba-5fa2-9127-413d6bff8d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3ln⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295391} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca76ff5-0227-56f5-94be-d80b462a5d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{ln⁡x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295528} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5899f6-d895-5413-bfee-0587b9cf1149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295802} {:answer/id #uuid "705c2710-b928-5586-a10e-67ac7d7acb16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295884} {:answer/id #uuid "bd595728-5d66-5fce-842a-829a9bf9e495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-3}{ln⁡|x|}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "705c2710-b928-5586-a10e-67ac7d7acb16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295884} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6439f4c-9689-5960-aa2c-ca05e0a5ed1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3f7435c-4593-5eb7-979c-b6475dc0ac4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-29831" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int -\\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $-3$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9f3ccb-57a5-54e7-9651-cd8e38e071bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}ln⁡(5K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295330} {:answer/id #uuid "137963fa-e7bf-5b99-b97d-16a6e2b83b62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295376} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba78d99-70e5-5576-9ab3-f6f5d63e5557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295610} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6cacd1-8561-54df-98e0-e7424109595d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-ln⁡(\\frac{5}{2}K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296168} {:answer/id #uuid "e35e2f64-0c17-5dfe-a27c-81e2452e3e98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ba78d99-70e5-5576-9ab3-f6f5d63e5557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295610} :answer-field/id #uuid "c450e82f-bb3f-51ae-bbf9-78580de51634" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eec65b66-7ead-5d6e-b179-5aacd453b9d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-29834" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $-\\frac{5}{2x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $-\\frac{5}{2}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{5}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{5}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡K \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}(ln⁡|x|+ln⁡K) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105716 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c865ec2-5bed-5892-966b-a586097be923" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Sum of a Reciprocal Function and a Power Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "298a44ce-4517-57c9-8784-5136e6d9c6af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{5}{x}^{11/3}+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295500} {:answer/id #uuid "2e55410d-693f-527e-b63c-efdd8027016a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}{x}^{4/3}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295534} {:answer/id #uuid "91074ff5-3842-537b-b69c-20abfee3093d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{5}{x}^{5/3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296046} {:answer/id #uuid "ae484638-462d-55ee-b006-7e7f7e36be1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}{x}^{7/3}+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296183} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b38dca-ffa5-5659-aa91-113d55a9cced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{5/3}+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6b38dca-ffa5-5659-aa91-113d55a9cced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{5/3}+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296395} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4ea97da-7b64-5d03-a1a8-9448f2925fe1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9a35970-b3a0-5bae-b863-30b3731d8d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-29885" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int ({x}^{2/3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{x})\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the sum rule and get\n\n$$\n\\int ({x}^{2/3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{x})\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\sqrt{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, using the power rule in the first term, and taking out the coefficient in the second term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\sqrt{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{2/3+1}}{\\frac{2}{3}+1}+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{5/3}+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105716 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c865ec2-5bed-5892-966b-a586097be923" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Sum of a Reciprocal Function and a Power Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0abba1e8-07a5-5982-8931-920e2b54ed11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295324} {:answer/id #uuid "14c3a82a-71bb-5b22-a1d7-5d3ffaa433a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295384} {:answer/id #uuid "218a6eff-a0a2-5775-878d-fb334d854a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295454} {:answer/id #uuid "5203ee4a-c762-565e-a1e7-1c414c5d786d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295738} {:answer/id #uuid "e96a5e23-083b-5d3f-bddc-50fd9a0aed8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14c3a82a-71bb-5b22-a1d7-5d3ffaa433a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295384} :answer-field/id #uuid "65380cf5-c8c6-595a-9584-e99d721414af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "77e80b12-44f5-5ee6-b586-d6d7898a0c32" :question/math-academy-id "q-29915" :question/problem "Find the integral of $\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}-5{x}^{-1}$ with respect to $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Simplifying the exponents and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}-5{x}^{-1})\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{5/2-3/2}}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115183 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e15de1-ee72-55ea-accb-1124f3a0ac0c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab6f005d-5267-58e5-84bf-a80272331a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,176" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395813} {:answer/id #uuid "7e3a07e3-abf9-52a3-90a9-53487e034b7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,641" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395814} {:answer/id #uuid "ce65e29e-1b9d-54dc-94af-379795c62563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,741" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395815} {:answer/id #uuid "198aafc0-07ef-5e53-9034-41f2a9658b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,746" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395816} {:answer/id #uuid "21c0594d-dee0-5fde-9687-2438c3bda33b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,764" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce65e29e-1b9d-54dc-94af-379795c62563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,741" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395815} :answer-field/id #uuid "d68ef59f-9eeb-59d5-b977-da3a30f3eafc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa37b599-4ad5-59f0-a1aa-7677947192cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-29938" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{5}{4}$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nNow, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{4}{5})=(53.130\\,1…)^{∘}≈{53.130}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${53.130}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={53.130}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={53.130}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={53.130}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/60/605af77803fb027c09833b50801fe4b8e6a42a2b623af94a732d0f2f222f9f4a.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{53.130}^{∘} \\\\  & ={126.870}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x={53.130}^{∘},{126.870}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈53.130\\cdot 126.870≈6\\,741.\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068760 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7614a05-f221-5fda-b491-6893f44a1a5f" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Rational Expressions with Multiple Common Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c4f1d16-78b3-5045-8bb5-5579fec4543a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295613} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3602d9-f3c5-5344-8c7b-288df52749cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11{y}^{2}}{k+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295709} {:answer/id #uuid "93b20eed-4388-595a-84d8-977910934218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{k+4}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296058} {:answer/id #uuid "aa7ed919-742c-5a4c-b988-a4265e3f6f06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{44(k+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296162} {:answer/id #uuid "e8492be0-a6b9-5d09-afa1-2e0f3bbeb2cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8492be0-a6b9-5d09-afa1-2e0f3bbeb2cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296502} :answer-field/id #uuid "17d9d487-4676-539c-9b1c-0c3a024bdb73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "964f86e1-4f13-55f4-a1ab-8caf3da2db2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-29970" :question/problem "If $\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}\\cdot Y=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}},$ then what is $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}\\cdot Y=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}},$ we have\n\n$$\nY=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}÷\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}.\n$$\n\nTo compute this quotient, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}÷\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{121y}{2(k+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2(k+2)\\cdot (k+2)}{11y\\cdot {y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{11\\cdot 11y}{2(k+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{2(k+2)}\\cdot (k+2)}{\\cancel{11y}\\cdot {y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{11\\cdot \\cancel{11y}}{\\cancel{2(k+2)}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot 11 & = \\\\ \\frac{22(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068860 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d78ba1c-5297-5540-afdc-d419d33e0cf7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation When the Solutions Are Rational"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9253c945-96c0-5170-ab31-77a3ad0f24d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 7, x_{1} + x_{2} = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066485} {:answer/id #uuid "a2710526-3439-5d45-a3cb-b94809df5d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 9, x_{1} + x_{2} = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066486} {:answer/id #uuid "b5eac8af-989a-5812-82cd-e6e80a244860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 7, x_{1} + x_{2} =-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066487} {:answer/id #uuid "cec6266a-38e0-5082-ae2c-0c20a9b251e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 7, x_{1} + x_{2} = 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066488} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b0d285-5968-53b9-b5b2-00fcaf5e3048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q =-11x_{1} + x_{2} = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066489}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d792d360-b68f-53ea-add1-82615c19183b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066484} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76d7ebef-b52d-5f30-ac74-d49866c8f6a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=7\\,,\\,\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401956} {:answer/id #uuid "d00f8b18-cb37-5c31-961a-3438330ad683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=7\\,,\\,\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401957} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0444d5-a369-5e49-89bd-c5ebc8c0237a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-11\\,\\,\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401958} {:answer/id #uuid "44f7e400-b932-5f1e-a0ab-bdb9a22e3846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=7\\,,\\,\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401959} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8857d6-881c-5549-bde0-3f1a4f61d2d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=9\\,,\\,\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76d7ebef-b52d-5f30-ac74-d49866c8f6a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=7\\,,\\,\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401956} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5fa6494-6274-595a-88b0-e033638de4a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c02e0e4e-c511-5b9e-9632-6db46dfe1bf6" :question/math-academy-id "q-30052" :question/problem "The equation ${x}^{2}-4x+4=9$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $\\,p+q,$ and the sum of the roots ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+4 & =9 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot x & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =5 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+{2}^{2})-4 & =5 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}-4 & =5 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=-2,q=9,$ and $p+q=7.$\n\nNow, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-2)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2+3=5 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2-3=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=5.$ Thus, the sum is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068860 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d78ba1c-5297-5540-afdc-d419d33e0cf7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation When the Solutions Are Rational"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f24ab63d-b4d8-50ae-8389-5045b51319bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=0\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401963} {:answer/id #uuid "6c1507f7-b1b3-56e3-a38c-1b563d660137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=-1\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401964} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d7f47d-c746-5735-9e9f-835593e46fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=-1\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401965} {:answer/id #uuid "aa922213-ad7d-513f-ad50-36362f5e8cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=1\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401966} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d50863-24e6-52af-928c-18cf82d1862f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=-1\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2d7f47d-c746-5735-9e9f-835593e46fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=-1\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401965} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f724395-4532-5650-9bbb-1b5a58b225fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "85741874-6828-5db4-a1ab-e8bb9b524df8" :question/math-academy-id "q-30055" :question/problem "The equation ${x}^{2}-2x+1=1$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $\\,pq\\,,$ and the sum of the roots ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+1 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot x\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $p=-1$ and $q=1.$ Thus, $pq=-1.$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\ |x-1| & =1 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =1\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =1-1=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0265ab4b-e69c-58cd-bb39-877b73f3298f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,11.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295275} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab493a4-b37f-5582-a8b9-4570920c001f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10.5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295861} {:answer/id #uuid "a1678182-8bd9-5b40-83a4-560b3c92dfe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296126} {:answer/id #uuid "ecdd579a-68d8-530f-aec2-948295be5064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296522} {:answer/id #uuid "f541b6c1-d1ab-5c71-9e34-c29b7b48861a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5.5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1678182-8bd9-5b40-83a4-560b3c92dfe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296126} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0972749-6df3-5836-b156-61b2f017bcaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "91d38446-45fe-5f55-b13c-726fa96fa8c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-30269" :question/problem "For the curve $y=2.5({1.5}^{x})+8$, find the point of intersection with the $y-$axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={1.5}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=2.5({1.5}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=2.5\\cdot 1=2.5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $8$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=2.5+8=10.5.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2.5({1.5}^{0})+8 \\\\  & =2.5\\cdot 1+8 \\\\  & =2.5+8 \\\\  & =10.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2.5({1.5}^{x})+8$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,10.5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064207 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdbfa506-08a1-5e43-b958-2ae373df775d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Linear Rational Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dbf09c3-18df-50a3-84f7-e6f47d2ef7a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295347} {:answer/id #uuid "6e3b22d0-1d8f-5569-b6bf-7b7599fb5580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295876} {:answer/id #uuid "962c31da-ae4b-5022-8358-2aec9b89c95b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296067} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e4ad65-9186-54e0-8792-0e53759b7716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296235} {:answer/id #uuid "fa999cda-1369-5935-898a-85571f00711c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8e4ad65-9186-54e0-8792-0e53759b7716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296235} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dfec598-1f66-5fc7-8005-3949a52a7718" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "60fb4a8e-0b44-516d-9ebb-a8d91494c67e" :question/math-academy-id "q-30368" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=3x+4.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+C & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e5493f0-49e5-5ac0-a64b-444f26facf5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295811} {:answer/id #uuid "cedf3ffd-b468-57c5-a1fb-5644942d8367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296352} {:answer/id #uuid "d228956c-dabb-5adf-b1f0-eea00077c044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296375} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e90daa-a470-5b92-aeb7-f90fc7f25a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296492} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3169b4-14de-5f3c-837e-5d327959c0c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d228956c-dabb-5adf-b1f0-eea00077c044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296375} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ef5eb88-b39d-569b-bc4c-949ed152d723" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fda2bca-a959-55aa-9acd-07aede112347" :question/math-academy-id "q-30873" :question/problem "For the curve $y=1.5({10}^{x})-8.5$, find the point of intersection with the $y-$axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={10}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=1.5({10}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=1.5\\cdot 1=1.5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $8.5$ units down, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=1.5-8.5=-7.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1.5({10}^{0})-8.5 \\\\  & =1.5\\cdot 1-8.5 \\\\  & =1.5-8.5 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=1.5({10}^{x})-8.5$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-7).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3eec195c-6549-5de2-b3f0-0417e5f518e7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Greater Than\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3924a0d6-844d-5dc6-83c6-7db4a2fb4e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<2$ or $x>4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295594} {:answer/id #uuid "440955b3-6ad7-5734-9cd2-e9f88aa0e954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2<x<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295651} {:answer/id #uuid "77034500-21c2-52e9-9478-7a3d41623c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-4$ or $x>-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295916} {:answer/id #uuid "7798a310-53c1-5f5f-8d13-7e74a6c7123a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4<x<-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295919} {:answer/id #uuid "ef80a80c-3c32-544a-b833-12476f1cf9e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "440955b3-6ad7-5734-9cd2-e9f88aa0e954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2<x<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295651} :answer-field/id #uuid "41198266-3a32-5b6f-aeaf-82b61bfcb718" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b9f08f5-9d21-5599-bebd-c942241b0925" :question/math-academy-id "q-31015" :question/problem "![](images/11/1126d885ff5000b4afdadffef7b47dec3b2932322dfa7b75d16bc00993218a0e.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-(x-2)(x-4)>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-(x-2)(x-4)>0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-(x-2)(x-4)\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/dc/dc39c970e292e38519e609367c9f4a3d810aaca99e7040bb6227ff45401b2efc.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $2<x<4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5bb394c3-cd7c-523d-88df-b1306d9d528f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c95a3fca-97ff-5e46-ad67-42c5b5b68ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bc/bc4c0c21c45fc3ed02d88150facf89718896a3e2992105f5a8a5d89b0355f9e0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390594} {:answer/id #uuid "73326b1a-1067-5996-991e-bb9d853a4e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6edb90966ce90a141e3fa4ee1a9eb3cf9c7131e3121e45f56b16bc4395076d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390595} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a74885-a855-53a6-a12b-5c1f9db59688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/06/062274f402396a61c76fae3a47c54de80ad31deac970c746f3f8f4627ce535f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390596} {:answer/id #uuid "dbeafe1a-8a14-5f3a-8d70-6e398d0ebc6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b3/b3f96cf981a9c127dd0e3c0fa3b73f548db8c640bbc4f0e048b4576356b69532.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390597} {:answer/id #uuid "44eec2a2-c1e5-5830-a6f1-3345cbd3b0c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b4/b48f88ac53107fddece776c81d91fc5684c7d6f06ea3b862fb99cd973eae2dc3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73326b1a-1067-5996-991e-bb9d853a4e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6edb90966ce90a141e3fa4ee1a9eb3cf9c7131e3121e45f56b16bc4395076d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390595} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5e7cf18-216c-59ac-8255-427849d7152c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6a50d86-9c2e-59fc-b26e-b4043bba0c39" :question/math-academy-id "q-31146" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of  $y={(\\frac{1}{12})}^{x}-4?$" :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{12})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/e2/e2f13711ca62a8a4a698e23bdfe78caca6df44d88ded27b88c517219dffbb866.png)\n\nNext, we shift this graph $4$ units down. \nThe horizontal asymptote under such a transformation becomes $y=-4,$ the $y$-intercept becomes $(0,-3).$\nThe resulting graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{12})}^{x}-4$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dc7f93220ac18ac5671b7b41de141940ca5296139b836d56755fdccf6164ed26.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064731 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f9548e6-28ef-5a97-ab04-dfb1b6e23c16" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cfff078-9fb4-5b67-bb26-7811957a40b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{ln⁡x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295341} {:answer/id #uuid "36c4e4d8-ea74-510a-8195-fcca114fa7c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295573} {:answer/id #uuid "4d71c1ee-dea6-5890-90dd-6cf50a8cd3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295711} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3c34e1-3b8b-568c-889c-57fe85dd59ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296256} {:answer/id #uuid "cd13943f-d3e1-5920-9b15-4d7240e4db43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc3c34e1-3b8b-568c-889c-57fe85dd59ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296256} :answer-field/id #uuid "b94705d1-73b6-521f-a07d-12831457766a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f474ed40-025d-552e-9e67-da66415ffc42" :question/math-academy-id "q-31245" :question/problem "$\\int -2{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Factoring out the constant $-2$ and calculating the resulting integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -2{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064177 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "980bb2fb-4bb5-5cbb-aea8-87010493c013" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Sum of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ddda862-700e-5259-8a9b-324825fe885d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}+\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295436} {:answer/id #uuid "3b3aafd2-10f8-5647-aeef-217f0b123766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295606} {:answer/id #uuid "4aaae1a6-c031-5bc5-a104-23401003f961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295691} {:answer/id #uuid "531024e5-c103-583c-92eb-e4200ac7a6df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{3x}-\\frac{{3}^{2x}}{ln⁡x}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295744} {:answer/id #uuid "56637843-7c20-5243-80d5-7c1405ee9de3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-3{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b3aafd2-10f8-5647-aeef-217f0b123766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295606} :answer-field/id #uuid "32505d16-6c49-5e59-9ed0-fdcb1b385a52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42f6b48b-f5fd-5245-b823-ea76a025e943" :question/math-academy-id "q-31252" :question/problem "$\\int (2{e}^{x}-{3}^{x}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{e}^{x}-{3}^{x}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115190 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbb56f0a-ead0-5794-9e33-7fe0e1f87dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "136ad750-eb9e-5055-a26a-c8cda8dba5ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+6csc⁡xcot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295374} {:answer/id #uuid "1cce689b-d66b-5419-b42f-1c935770579a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-6csc⁡xcot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295433} {:answer/id #uuid "4c7cbd2b-769e-5c2a-8198-1408890da6d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2+6csc⁡xcot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295702} {:answer/id #uuid "be094b01-e8a4-5075-a548-43ad86b83a02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+6csc⁡xcot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296269} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c1681e-054f-5ee6-ac7d-f24a14f0c567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+6csc⁡xcot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0c1681e-054f-5ee6-ac7d-f24a14f0c567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+6csc⁡xcot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296463} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed22d9b2-1f65-5896-a2a0-0c98a0de1f14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "683c5003-d2ff-5388-892e-f05923834b32" :question/math-academy-id "q-31283" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={x}^{2}-6csc⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-6csc⁡x) \\\\  & =2x-6(-csc⁡xcot⁡x) \\\\  & =2x+6csc⁡xcot⁡x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064282 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ef81a0d-5a3a-5d7c-83e9-7d9ed0e8c5c6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074a9ec9-6bfa-51c3-b293-a2e6c7634c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295302} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8e6cf9-9f11-5e02-a08b-b0ce3fd9f2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295608} {:answer/id #uuid "64d5cac3-e49f-55a4-a517-b40d5927cff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295841} {:answer/id #uuid "748fff53-6b97-5ddb-a2b7-7573ce8fffb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295904} {:answer/id #uuid "c09b0310-1b57-58cd-bbcf-bbf81a785509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b8e6cf9-9f11-5e02-a08b-b0ce3fd9f2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295608} :answer-field/id #uuid "67494433-5316-509e-bbfc-ae0d8a99e4af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d5db746-ab9b-5b5a-bc25-31e21fc4632d" :question/math-academy-id "q-31338" :question/problem "Find the slope of $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\pi -3x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is given by ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,u=2\\pi -3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi -3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3ee0d5d-0821-5f6b-b613-6b814e65df50" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using Multiple Instances of the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "143d951a-1e7a-598c-ab01-6ac4eebb972d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295381} {:answer/id #uuid "80ac022d-2fa2-5312-b6bb-20edccc427c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{cot}^{3}⁡1{sec}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295959} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd53f52-57a0-58c2-84e5-51e7836dcaa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{cot}^{2}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296358} {:answer/id #uuid "df819a99-e827-5e8d-8038-4bdc1f81e2b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\operatorname{cot}⁡1{sec}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296451} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c37201-fff5-5a86-9be9-3c9d741d2eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "143d951a-1e7a-598c-ab01-6ac4eebb972d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295381} :answer-field/id #uuid "c18783dd-cacf-52c8-94df-17f7aaad8258" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "60550674-e8b4-5f20-9b57-5b71c82e6894" :question/math-academy-id "q-31361" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ when $f(x)={cot}^{4}⁡(5x-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{4},\\,u=\\operatorname{cot}⁡v,\\,v=5x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=4{u}^{3},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-{csc}^{2}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡v)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x-1))^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡(5x-1))\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-20{cot}^{3}⁡(5x-1){csc}^{2}⁡(5x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${f}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-20{cot}^{3}⁡(5(0)-1){csc}^{2}⁡(5(0)-1) \\\\  & =-20{cot}^{3}⁡(-1){csc}^{2}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-20(-{cot}^{3}⁡1){csc}^{2}⁡1 \\\\  & =20{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "617f0fe6-7998-5d77-b11f-55a0eca11440" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84ebbe2d-74ec-5380-9a07-702d026d313d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/73/73590d55299fc519582252723f34bfa143818da7f03a16f7aeb8d208217ee3d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066589} {:answer/id #uuid "00accd83-f7f9-57bb-aa6e-78f38f349bb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b5/b53396e5736d8e09a11acef07594950ee50a0c027e190f0c8eb85880cf721494.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066590} {:answer/id #uuid "0e7e45dc-8e5a-5e38-a11e-07a10f544e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c8/c8652694c97439511e40